@model Attendance.Admin.Controllers.CreatedEmployeeViewModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Employee created";
}

<h1>Created @Model.FullName</h1>

<div class="warning">
    <p>
        <strong>This password is shown once.</strong> Only its hash was stored, so
        there is no way to display it again. If it is lost before it reaches
        @Model.FullName, issue a new one rather than looking for this page.
    </p>
    <p>
        Hand it over in person. Do not send it by email or message, and do not
        write it in a ticket.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>Sign-in details</h2>

<dl>
    <dt>User identifier</dt>
    <dd><code>@Model.UserId</code></dd>

    <dt>Initial password</dt>
    <dd class="secret"><code>@Model.InitialPassword</code></dd>
</dl>

<p>
    The hyphens are part of the password. They are there to make it readable when
    copied off a screen, and characters that look alike in handwriting &mdash;
    <code>0</code> and <code>O</code>, <code>1</code> and <code>l</code> &mdash;
    have been left out for the same reason.
</p>

<h2>@Model.FullName cannot clock in yet</h2>

<p>
    Two things are still outstanding, and until both are done they will be
    refused at the door:
</p>

<ol>
    <li>
        An authenticator has to be enrolled and proven. Do it now, while they are
        with you &mdash; it needs their phone.
    </li>
    <li>
        Their device has to register from the app and be approved under
        <a asp-controller="Devices" asp-action="Pending">Devices</a>.
    </li>
</ol>

<form method="post" asp-action="Enrol">
    <input type="hidden" name="mobileUserId" value="@Model.MobileUserId" />
    <input type="hidden" name="userId" value="@Model.UserId" />
    <input type="hidden" name="replaceExisting" value="false" />
    <button type="submit">Enrol an authenticator</button>
</form>

<p><a asp-action="Index">Back to employees</a></p>
